TITLE:: NdefRackGUI
summary:: the rack window - modules, cables, selection, and the toolbar
categories:: Libraries>NdefRack
related:: Classes/NdefRack, Classes/NdefRackModuleView, Classes/NdefRackHintBar, Classes/NdefRackDisplay

DESCRIPTION::
Opened with link::Classes/NdefRack#*gui::; one at a time.

code::
s.boot;
NdefRack.gui;
NdefRack.make(\wave);
NdefRack.make(\lfo).set(\lo, -0.5, \hi, 0.5);
NdefRack.connect(\lfo, \wave, \oct);
::

Modules are link::Classes/NdefRackModuleView::s on a scrolling canvas. Every
cable is drawn on one transparent view made before every module and never
raised, so the cables run behind the modules and never cover a label, a value or
a port: flat curves from an output on a module's right edge to an input on
another's left edge, leaving and arriving level - orange for audio, blue for
control (link::#-rateColor::). A cable going backwards loops out of its output
and into its input; one with more than one channel is twice as wide, with a dark
line down its middle. The cables of the selected modules, the cable under the
mouse and the cable being dragged are drawn again on a transparent view above
everything, so a selected module's cables can be followed end to end. The
window keeps nothing of the patch:
it listens to link::Classes/NdefRack::, marks what changed, and draws it at the
next frame, thirty times a second. What it keeps is its own - the selection and
the drag in progress.

At every frame it also reads the output of each control rate module from the
server's shared memory - the lights by an output, the value arriving at a
patched control and the band of where it has been - and asks each scope and spectrum module for its picture
(link::Classes/NdefRackDisplay::). The live values need a local server, for its
shared memory; the pictures need only a running one.

subsection:: Using it

table::
## strong::+ module:: || a menu of the library by category; or right click the empty rack to put one there. buses has bus and the modules that write to one - send, throw, sendpan, replace, blend, insert - each made on no bus: pick one in its drop-down
## strong::patch as code:: || link::Classes/NdefRack#*patchDocument:: - the rack as code, in a new document
## strong::save patch:: || link::Classes/NdefRack#*savePatch::, to the patches folder
## strong::load patch:: || the patches on disk, newest first
## strong::arrange:: || the modules left to right, the way the signal flows, each column ordered to uncross the cables; modules with no cables in a row underneath - see link::Classes/NdefRackLayout::. With two or more modules selected, only them, by the layout rules - link::#-arrangeModules::
## strong::learn:: || with the Modality toolkit installed: click a control, then move an element of any MKtl - see link::Classes/NdefRackKtl::
## strong::cables:: || how strongly the cables are drawn - link::#*cableAlpha::
## strong::+ comment:: || a box of text in the middle of what is seen - see link::Classes/NdefRackCommentView::
## strong::-:: strong::100%:: strong::+:: || zoom out, back to 100%, in - link::#-zoom::
## strong::fit::, strong::centre:: || everything in view; back to the middle of the patch - link::#-fitAll::, link::#-centre::
## strong::flow chart:: v || how the cables are drawn: a flow chart, curves or straight lines - link::#*cableStyle::
## strong::rec::, strong::recorder:: || with BusRecorder installed: start and stop a take; open its window
## strong::hints:: || folds away the bar along the bottom that says what the mouse is over
::

The buttons come in groups - strong::+ module:: and strong::arrange::; the patch
as a whole; strong::learn::; recording; how the cables are drawn - and a group
whose quark is missing is not there.

On a module: click the title or the plain face to select it and drag to move
it - with every other selected module; the play button plays an audio rate
module, the x deletes the module. Drag a bar left and right, shift for fine,
click its value to type one, double click to reset; click a switch; click and
drag across an array; hold a button - see link::Classes/NdefRackModuleView::.
Right click for the rest - among it, for a module that can have them, its
voices. A module with voices shows how many after its name, code::×4::.

subsection:: Patching

table::
## strong::gesture:: || strong::does::
## drag from a module's output - the right half of the main row - to a row || a cable into that control; the whole row counts, not only its port
## ... to a module's title || a cable into its first free main input of the cable's rate, else its first main input - link::Classes/NdefRackModuleView#-titleInput::
## drag from an input's port to another module || a cable from that module's output; anywhere on the module counts
## drag a plugged input's port away || pick the cable up: drop it on another row to move it, on the empty rack to unplug it, back on its port to leave it
## click an output or a port, let go, click a target || the same cable with no button held; it follows the mouse in between - link::#-armedCable::
## escape, while a cable is loose || let it go: nothing is plugged or unplugged
## right click a cable on the empty rack || strong::unplug:: it, first in the menu
::

While a cable is loose, every port it can land on grows, and the row it would
land on is outlined and tinted in its colour (link::#-dragColor::) - the output,
for a cable from an input. A cable it would replace is drawn dashed. The hint
bar says what letting go would do - code::lfo → oct of lpf::, then
"replaces env", "audio into a control input", "into itself, one block late" or
"only the first voice arrives" where they apply
(link::Classes/NdefRackModuleView#-dropHint::). Over a target the loose end
snaps to the port it would land on. Near the edge of what you can see, the rack
scrolls. Nothing is refused: the model refuses nothing either.

Where two modules overlap, a drop goes to the one on top - the one made,
rebuilt or dragged last (link::#-zOrder::). When a module arrives on top of
another, the hint bar says "modules overlap - arrange tidies them"; nothing is
moved on its own.

subsection:: The cable under the mouse

Rest the mouse on a cable for link::#*previewDelay:: seconds and a small picture
of what it carries opens beside it: a row per channel - per voice - with where it
comes from and where it goes, and the level of each row. Moving the mouse closes
it; resting again opens it again. It closes as well the moment its cable is gone
- unplugged, or either module deleted, in the window or in code - and while
anything is being dragged. An audio cable shows 30 milliseconds of
each channel, up to 16, triggered as channel 1 rises through zero; the window
captures it with an link::Classes/NdefRackDisplay:: of kind code::\probe::
(link::#-probe::), made when the picture opens and freed a second after it
closes - at once when its module goes - so it needs a running server. A control cable shows the last
link::#*historyLength:: frames of each channel, read from shared memory with the
live values, so it needs a local one. A mono cable spread over voices is one
row. The hint bar describes the cable at the same time.

subsection:: Selection and keys

table::
## strong::gesture:: || strong::does::
## click a title or a face || select that module alone
## shift-click || add it to the selection, or take it out
## drag on the empty rack || a band: select what it touches; with shift, add to the selection
## drag a selected module || move the whole selection
## cmd-c, cmd-x, cmd-v || copy, cut, paste where the mouse is
## cmd-d || duplicate, the clipboard untouched
## backspace, delete || delete the selection
## cmd-z || bring the last delete back
## cmd-a, escape || select every module, select nothing; escape first lets a loose cable go
::

Copying takes the cables between the copied modules along; a cable from or to
anything outside the copy stays behind - see link::Classes/NdefRack#*copy::.
The toolbar does not take the keyboard, so the keys always reach the rack.

subsection:: Opening and closing

Opening the window starts link::Classes/NdefRack#*watch::; closing it stops the
watching again, unless something had started it before, and frees every
display. Its frames run on a link::Classes/SkipJack::, so cmd + period does not
freeze it.


CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
Use link::Classes/NdefRack#*gui:: instead, which keeps one window.

METHOD:: cableAlpha
How opaque the cables are, 0.1 to 1. 1.0: they run behind the modules, so they
hide nothing but the grid.

METHOD:: showReads
Whether the dashed lines for Ndefs read inside source functions are drawn, into
the left edge of the reading module's title. true.

METHOD:: frameRate
Frames a second. 30; takes effect for the next window.

METHOD:: voiceChoices
The numbers of voices a module's menu offers. code::#[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 12, 16]::.

METHOD:: cableStyle
How cables are drawn: code::\routed:: - a flow chart, straight runs at right
angles round the modules (link::Classes/NdefRackRouter::), the default -
code::\curved:: or code::\straight::. The toolbar's menu sets it, and follows it
when it is set from code; the mouse finds a cable where it is drawn whichever
way. In the flow chart a click on a cable selects it and a drag moves where it
bends (link::Classes/NdefRack#*bend::).

METHOD:: straightCables
true sets link::#*cableStyle:: to code::\straight::, false to code::\curved::;
answers whether it is code::\straight::. From before the flow chart.

METHOD:: palette
The colours a module, a cable or a network box can be marked with, name ->
link::Classes/Color::: red, yellow, green, teal, indigo, purple, pink, white -
none of them audio's orange or control's blue.

METHOD:: paletteNames
The palette's names, in the menus' order.

METHOD:: zoomSteps
The zooms link::#-zoomStep:: goes through, the toolbar's strong::-:: and
strong::+::, cmd-minus, cmd-plus and cmd with the wheel.
code::#[0.25, 0.33, 0.5, 0.67, 0.75, 0.9, 1, 1.1, 1.25, 1.5, 2]::.

METHOD:: historyLength
How many frames of a control signal the picture over a cable shows. 60 - two
seconds at 30 frames a second.

METHOD:: previewDelay
How many seconds the mouse rests on a cable before its picture opens. 0.5.

METHOD:: colRec
The red of recording: the rec button, the dot on a recorded module.

METHOD:: colAudio
The colour of audio: its cables, ports and rate line, orange.

METHOD:: colControl
The colour of control: its cables, ports and rate line, sky blue.

METHOD:: colAccent
Near-white: the selection, a loose cable, a drop target, play on.

METHOD:: colBg
METHOD:: colPanel
METHOD:: colTitle
METHOD:: colDim
METHOD:: cableColors
The other colours, read by the module views and the hint bar too.
code::cableColors:: is the palette of the scope's traces.


INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: window
The link::Classes/Window::.

METHOD:: views
The module views, key -> link::Classes/NdefRackModuleView::.

METHOD:: hints
The link::Classes/NdefRackHintBar::.

METHOD:: canvas
The link::Classes/UserView:: the modules sit on, inside the scroll.

METHOD:: zOrder
The module keys, the one on top first: the module made, rebuilt or dragged most
recently. A cable being dropped is tested against the modules in this order.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Array:: of link::Classes/Symbol::s, a copy.

METHOD:: browser
The module library down the left of the window.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/NdefRackBrowser::

METHOD:: showBrowser
Show the module library, or fold it away - the toolbar's strong::modules::, cmd-b. Remembered in link::Classes/NdefRackBrowser#*shown::.
ARGUMENT:: flag
true shows it

METHOD:: front
Bring it to the front.

METHOD:: close
Close it.

METHOD:: isClosed
Whether it has been closed.

METHOD:: selection
The selected modules' keys, in the order they were selected.

METHOD:: isSelected
Whether that module is selected.

METHOD:: select
Select these modules, and only these.
ARGUMENT:: keys
an Array of Symbols.

METHOD:: selectAll
Select every module.

METHOD:: deselect
Select nothing.

METHOD:: zoom
How big the rack is drawn, 0.25 to 2; 1 is 100%. Places, bends and comments stay
in the rack's own coordinates whatever the zoom; the window's views go where it
puts them.
ARGUMENT:: z
The zoom, clipped to the first and last of link::#*zoomSteps::.
ARGUMENT:: about
The point of the rack that stays where it is on the screen: the middle of what
is seen when nil.

METHOD:: zoomStep
The next of link::#*zoomSteps:: in or out.
ARGUMENT:: dir
1 in, -1 out.
ARGUMENT:: about
As for link::#-zoom::.

METHOD:: arrangeModules
Lay out some modules by the layout rules (link::Classes/NdefRackLayout#-tidy::), from the top left corner of where they are
now: the main audio path on a row, a row under it for each other part. Only the
cables between them count; no other module moves, and the bends of the cables
between them go. Key strong::a::, the toolbar's strong::arrange:: with a
selection, or "arrange ..." in a selected module's menu.
ARGUMENT:: keys
the names; default the selection. Fewer than two: nothing happens.

METHOD:: fitAll
Every module and comment in view, at a zoom no bigger than 1, and centred.

METHOD:: centre
Scroll back to the middle of the patch - the box round every module and comment
- at the zoom there is.

METHOD:: addComment
A comment, selected, with a field open to write in it when it has no text.
ARGUMENT:: point
Its top left on the rack; the middle of what is seen when nil.
ARGUMENT:: text
What it says.
RETURNS:: its id

METHOD:: deleteComment
Delete a comment.
ARGUMENT:: id
Its id.

METHOD:: selectComment
Select a comment, nil for none; backspace deletes the selected one.
ARGUMENT:: id
Its id.

METHOD:: selectedComment
The selected comment's id, or nil.

METHOD:: commentViews
The comments' views, id -> link::Classes/NdefRackCommentView::.

METHOD:: setCompact
Make modules compact, or show every control of them - the compact button and the
menu's strong::compact::.
ARGUMENT:: keys
The modules.
ARGUMENT:: flag
true for compact.

METHOD:: colorMenu
A link::Classes/Menu:: of the palette, strong::none:: first, the current colour
ticked.
ARGUMENT:: current
The colour name there is, or nil.
ARGUMENT:: action
Called with the name picked, nil for none.

METHOD:: paletteColor
A colour name as a link::Classes/Color::; nil for nil or a name not in the
palette.
ARGUMENT:: name
A colour name.

METHOD:: boxSelection
A network box round the selected modules - with none selected, an empty one in
the middle of what is seen. cmd-g.
RETURNS:: its id

METHOD:: addBox
A network box, selected.
ARGUMENT:: point
Its top left; the middle of what is seen when nil.
ARGUMENT:: size
Its width and height. 400 @ 300.
ARGUMENT:: name
Its name; "network n" when nil.
ARGUMENT:: color
A colour name. code::\teal::.
RETURNS:: its id

METHOD:: deleteBox
Delete a box; what was in it stays.
ARGUMENT:: id
Its id.

METHOD:: selectBox
Select a box, nil for none; backspace deletes the selected one.
ARGUMENT:: id
Its id.

METHOD:: selectedBox
The selected box's id, or nil.

METHOD:: renameBoxField
A field over a box's title to name it, as a double click on the title opens:
return or a click elsewhere keeps the name, escape leaves it as it was.
ARGUMENT:: id
Its id.

METHOD:: boxTitleH
How tall a box's title strip is: 22. Dragging the title moves the box.

METHOD:: selectCable
Select a cable, as the click on it does: it is drawn above the modules, with a
handle on each run a drag moves; backspace unplugs it. nil selects none.
ARGUMENT:: cable
An Event as link::Classes/NdefRack#*cables:: has them - code::(from:, to:, param:)::
- or nil.

METHOD:: selectedCable
The selected cable, an Event, or nil. It goes when it is unplugged or a module at
its end goes.

METHOD:: unbendCable
Give a cable's way back to the window: link::Classes/NdefRack#*bend:: with nil.
ARGUMENT:: c
An Event as link::Classes/NdefRack#*cables:: has them.

METHOD:: actionKeys
What a module's menu acts on: the selection when the module is part of one,
otherwise the module alone.

METHOD:: deleteModules
link::Classes/NdefRack#*removeAll::, and the hint bar says what went.

METHOD:: undoDelete
link::Classes/NdefRack#*undoRemove::, and the modules brought back selected.

METHOD:: copyModules
METHOD:: cutModules
link::Classes/NdefRack#*copy:: and link::Classes/NdefRack#*cut::.

METHOD:: pasteModules
link::Classes/NdefRack#*paste::, and what was pasted selected.
ARGUMENT:: at
where the top left of the group lands; nil for beside the originals.

METHOD:: duplicateModules
link::Classes/NdefRack#*duplicate::, and the copies selected.

METHOD:: setRecorded
What a module's record menu does: link::Classes/NdefRack#*setRecorded:: for every
audio module among keys, and a note in the hint bar.
ARGUMENT:: keys
the modules - the selection, from a menu.
ARGUMENT:: flag
true to record them, false to stop.

METHOD:: setVoices
What the voices menu does: link::Classes/NdefRack#*setVoices:: for every one of
the modules that can have voices, and a note in the hint bar.
ARGUMENT:: keys
the modules - the selection, from a menu.
ARGUMENT:: n
how many voices.

METHOD:: liveValue
METHOD:: liveValues
What a control rate proxy's output was at the last frame - one channel, or all of
them; nil for audio rate.

METHOD:: liveHistory
The last link::#*historyLength:: frames of every control rate module's output,
key -> a List of frames, newest last. Needs a local server.

METHOD:: liveRange
The lowest and highest value a control rate proxy sent on one channel over the
last link::#*historyLength:: frames - what a patched row's band shows.
ARGUMENT:: proxy
the source Ndef.
ARGUMENT:: ch
the channel, 0 for the first.
RETURNS:: code::[lo, hi]::, or nil while there is no history: an audio source, no local server.

METHOD:: rateColor
The colour of what a module's output carries: link::#*colAudio:: or
link::#*colControl::, dim for an unknown key.
ARGUMENT:: key
the module's key.

METHOD:: cableColor
The colour of the cable into an input: the rate colour of the module it comes
from (link::#-rateColor::). A plugged port fills with it, so audio arriving at a
control shows at a glance.
ARGUMENT:: to
the module the cable goes into.
ARGUMENT:: param
the input.

METHOD:: dragTarget
Where a loose cable would land, code::[key, param]::, or nil: an input's row,
or - dropped on a title - the input link::Classes/NdefRackModuleView#-titleInput::
answers; code::[key, \out]:: for a cable started from an input.

METHOD:: cableMode
What a loose cable is doing: code::\out:: (from an output), code::\in:: (from an
empty input), code::\carry:: (a plugged one picked up); nil while none is loose.

METHOD:: dragColor
The colour of what the loose cable would carry: the rate of the module it comes
from - for a cable from an input, of the module under it; nil while none is loose.

METHOD:: armedCable
The loose cable a click left on the mouse, an Event with its code::\mode::,
code::\from::, code::\to::, code::\param:: and code::\point:: - a copy - or nil. A
press and release in place (under 4 pixels) on an output or an input arms it;
its end follows the mouse each frame with no button held, and the next click
lands it - on a row, a title, or an output for a cable from an input. A click on
the empty rack, a right click or escape lets it go; so does learn mode, or either
module going. A cable picked up from a plugged port this way stays plugged when
let go.

METHOD:: hoveredCable
The cable under the mouse at the last frame, an Event as link::Classes/NdefRack#*cables:: has
them, or nil - nil over any module, since the cable runs behind it there; nil too
while anything is being dragged, and for a cable the node maps no longer hold.

METHOD:: previewCable
The cable whose picture is open, or nil: the hovered cable, once the mouse has rested
on it for link::#*previewDelay::.

METHOD:: cableHintAt
What the hint bar says over a cable at a point of the rack, or nil when no cable is
within a few pixels of it, the point is inside a module, or it is within 14
pixels of a cable's end, where the port is what the mouse is on.
ARGUMENT:: point
a link::Classes/Point:: in the rack's coordinates.

METHOD:: probe
The link::Classes/NdefRackDisplay:: capturing the audio cable under the mouse, or nil.
The window makes it and frees it.
